archar(4000)),\',\',\'\'),\'$\',\'\') as AppValueRangeTo, NULLIF(os.MarketValue,0) AS \'EstimatedMarketValue\',\n    CASE WHEN UC.Cityid is null THEN \'Rural\' ELSE \'Urban\' END as Region,\n    CASE WHEN SUBSTRING(p.postalcode, 2, 1) = \'0\' then \'Rural\' ELSE \'Urban\' END as PostalRural, EL.TTLORIGINVOICEAMOUNT as Price,\n    EL.TTLVENDORCOST as Cost,\n    os.AppraiserGrossTAT,\n    os.AppraiserNetTAT,\n    EXID.expid as EXCEPTIONID,\n    SD.PROPERTYVALUE as \'CleanAppraisedValue\',\n    r.FirstName + \' \' + r.LastName AS "Requester Name"\n    DataMart.dbo.OrderSummary AS os\n    left JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.[order] AS o on os.orderid = o.orderid Left Join\n    CAPS.dbo.[Requester] as r on o.RequesterID = r.RequesterID left JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.Service AS svc ON(svc.ServiceID = os.ServiceID) left JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.OrderStatus AS ostat ON(ostat.OrderStatusID = o.OrderStatusID) left JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.Property AS p ON(p.PropertyID = os.PropertyID) left JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.AppraiserInfo AS ai ON (ai.gp_vendorID = os.AppraiserFirm) LEFT JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.Province AS prov ON(prov.ProvinceID = p.ProvinceID) LEFT JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.City AS c ON(c.CityID = p.CityID) LEFT JOIN\n    CAPS.dbo.Street AS st ON(st.StreetID = p.StreetID) LEFT JOIN\n    Caps.cruser.UrbanCity UC ON C.Cityid = UC.Cityid LEFT JOIN\n    fbc.dbo.TGO_EXTENDEDORDERS_LINE EL WITH (NOLOCK) ON O.Orderid = EL.Ordernumber LEFT JOIN\n    (select [ORDERNUMBER], MAX(expID) as expid from (SELECT\n    [ORDERNUMBER] ,isnull([EXCEPTIONID],0) as expID\n    FROM [FBC].[dbo].[TGO_EXTENDEDORDERSERVICES]) dd group by dd.[ORDERNUMBER]) EXID on EXID.ORDERNUMBER = os.orderid\n    LEFT JOIN (select ORDERID, max(PROPERTYVALUE) as PROPERTYVALUE from TrendPoint.dbo.SourceData where DATAID = 0 and CLEANDATA_FLAG in (0,1)group by orderID having\n    COUNT(*) < 3) SD on SD.ORDERID = os.OrderID\n    LEFT JOIN (select * from CAPS.dbo.AppraisalDataExtract where WorkingCopy = 0)ADE on ADE.OrderID = os.OrderID\n    WHERE os.CompletionDate >= \'2012-01-01\' and os.OrderID in (2427915, 2427917,2437863,2437880,2437630)\n    \n\n\n\n### Answer:\n\nYou might want to open execution plan for this query to find the most\nexpensive Operators and start from there if there is no other process blocking\nthis query.  maybe Missing index and too many joins\n\n"""'}], 'response': "The SQL query provided appears to be a complex query that retrieves various information about orders, including order details, customer information, appraisal data, and more. The query uses several joins and subqueries to retrieve the desired data.\n\nTo improve the performance of this query, I would suggest the following:\n\n1. **Indexing**: Create indexes on columns used in the WHERE clause, such as `os.CompletionDate` and `os.OrderID`. This can help speed up the query by reducing the number of rows that need to be scanned.\n2. **Simplify joins**: The query uses several left joins, which can be expensive operations. Consider simplifying the joins by using inner joins or subqueries instead.\n3. **Optimize subqueries**: The query contains several subqueries, including one that retrieves the maximum `PROPERTYVALUE` for each order. Consider rewriting these subqueries to use window functions or common table expressions (CTEs) instead.\n4. **Use efficient data types**: The query uses various data types, such as `datetime`, `int`, and `varchar`. Ensure that you are using the most efficient data type for each column to reduce storage requirements and improve query performance.\n5. **Avoid unnecessary calculations**: The query performs several calculations, including aggregations and string manipulations. Consider avoiding these calculations or optimizing them using indexes or other techniques.\n\nHere is a rewritten version of the query that incorporates some of these suggestions:\n```sql\nSELECT \n    svc.[Name] AS 'Type',\n    os.OrderID,\n    os.CustomerID,\n    os.Transit,\n    ...\nFROM \n    DataMart.dbo.OrderSummary AS os\n    INNER JOIN CAPS.dbo.[order] AS o ON os.orderid = o.orderid\n    INNER JOIN CAPS.dbo.Requester AS r ON o.RequesterID = r.RequesterID\n    ...\nWHERE \n    os.CompletionDate >= '2012-01-01' AND os.OrderID IN (2427915, 2427917, 2437863, 2437880, 2437630)\n```\nNote that this is just a rewritten version of the query and may not necessarily improve performance. It's always a good idea to test the query with different indexes and optimizations to determine what works best for your specific use case.", 'time': 4.607081413269043}
