ucation_status     = \'Unknown\')\n        or(cd_marital_status       = \'W\' and cd_education_status     = \'Advanced Degree\'))\nand     hd_buy_potential like \'1001-5000%\'\nand     ca_gmt_offset           = -7\ngroup by cc_call_center_id,cc_name,cc_manager,cd_marital_status,cd_education_status\norder by sum(cr_net_loss) desc;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Suggestions:\n### Suggestion 1:\n"""Here is a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using various strategies to optimize its performance and readability. Query Rewrite 1 transforms filter conditions in the WHERE clause into ON clauses for JOIN operations, making the query more hierarchical and easier to optimize. Query Rewrite 2 eliminates the outer projection layer by applying GROUP BY and aggregate functions directly to the data source. Query Rewrite 3 prioritizes grouping operations at the earliest stage of execution to minimize workload on subsequent operations like JOIN. Query Rewrite 4 moves repeated function calls or operations outside of loops, while Query Rewrite 5 simplifies expressions and calculations to reduce complexity and execution time. Finally, Query Rewrite 6 transforms outer query conditions into the WHERE clause of subqueries when these conditions do not affect aggregations or window functions."""\n\nQuery Rewrite Rules:\n### Rule FILTER_INTO_JOIN:\n"""Case 1:\n**Conditions**: This SQL query rewrite rule applies when a filter condition is placed on the result set of an INNER JOIN operation. The filter\'s expressions do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join but can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs.\n**Transformations**: If a SQL query contains an INNER JOIN with a WHERE clause that can be logically associated only with columns from one side of the join (either left or right), move these conditions into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN or as a WHERE clause